HTB — The Last Dance
Category	Difficulty	Platform	Date compiled
Cryptography	Very Easy	Hack The Box	March 2026
Skills demonstrated: stream-cipher analysis, keystream-reuse (two-time pad) attack, known-plaintext XOR recovery.

Summary:
source.py encrypts a known plaintext message and the secret flag with ChaCha20 using the same key and nonce.
Reusing a (key, nonce) pair in a stream cipher produces the same keystream for both, which is a two-time pad. 
XORing the known plaintext with its ciphertext recovers the keystream, and XORing that keystream against the flag ciphertext recovers the flag. No key recovery is needed.

Analysis:
out.txt provides the nonce and two ciphertexts:
Nonce: c4a66edfe80227b4fa24d431 (12 bytes) — not even needed for the attack
Encrypted message (ct1, 156 bytes): hex string in out.txt
Encrypted flag (ct2, 51 bytes): hex string in out.txt

Known plaintext (hardcoded in source.py):
Our counter agencies have intercepted your messages and a lot of your agent's identities have been exposed. In a matter of days all of them will be captured
The known plaintext is exactly 156 bytes — the same length as ct1 — which confirms it maps to the message ciphertext and lets the full keystream be recovered.

Attack:
For a stream cipher, ciphertext = plaintext ⊕ keystream. With the keystream reused:
keystream = plaintext ⊕ ct1          # recover keystream from the known message
flag      = keystream[:len(ct2)] ⊕ ct2   # apply it to the flag ciphertext
Python
python
pt  = b"Our counter agencies have intercepted your messages and a lot of your agent's identities have been exposed. In a matter of days all of them will be captured"
ct1 = bytes.fromhex(open("ct1.hex").read().strip())
ct2 = bytes.fromhex(open("ct2.hex").read().strip())
ks   = bytes(a ^ b for a, b in zip(pt, ct1))
flag = bytes(a ^ b for a, b in zip(ks[:len(ct2)], ct2))
print(flag.decode())
CyberChef (no-code alternative)
Input the known plaintext → To Hex (no delimiter).
XOR with the ct1 hex → full keystream (hex).
Truncate to the first 2 × len(ct2) hex chars (102 here).
XOR that keystream prefix with the ct2 hex.
From Hex → UTF-8 → the flag.

