Reproduction note:
Running the attack on the data as transcribed in the original draft produces one wrong byte: at the position of the 3 in ...57R3aM... ("stream"), the XOR yields a control byte (0x03) instead of 0x33. 
Every other byte decodes correctly, so the flag is still obvious by inspection — but the published data doesn't reproduce it exactly.
The known plaintext there (...agencies have...) is correct, so the slip is a single hex digit in ct1 or ct2 at byte index 21. 
Re-copy that byte from the actual out.txt. For a crypto write-up the whole value is reproducibility, so paste the hex verbatim and re-run before publishing — never hand-correct the flag while leaving the data that produced it wrong.

Root cause:
Nonce reuse in a stream cipher (CWE-323, Reusing a Nonce/Key Pair in Encryption). ChaCha20's security requires a unique nonce per encryption under a given key (RFC 8439).
Encrypting two messages with the same (key, nonce) reuses the keystream, so ct1 ⊕ ct2 = pt1 ⊕ pt2, and any known plaintext exposes the other message.

Defensive takeaways:
Never reuse a (key, nonce) pair. Use a random 96-bit nonce per message, or a strict monotonic counter, and rotate keys before the counter can wrap.
Prefer an AEAD construction (ChaCha20-Poly1305) so reuse is both avoided and detectable through authentication.
Catch this in review/static analysis: a hardcoded or constant nonce passed to a stream cipher is the signature.

Key lessons:
Keystream reuse turns a stream cipher into a two-time pad; one known plaintext breaks both messages.
XOR is its own inverse — the property that makes the attack trivial.
Verify a recovered flag by re-deriving it from the published data, not by eyeballing — a one-byte data error is easy to miss when the flag is "obvious."

